<Post title="What 32 website rebuilds taught us about speed" description="Why a 7-day timeline produces better websites than a 7-week one — the system behind Trayaam's fast delivery, and what we learned along the way." path="/blog/7-day-website-rebuild-what-we-learned" date="May 2026" readtime="5 min read" category="Process">
  <p>A seven-day rebuild sounds like a compromise. In our experience it's the opposite — constraint forces clarity. Here's what 32 projects taught us.</p>
  <h2>Audit first removes the guessing</h2>
  <p>Most timelines balloon during an open-ended "discovery" phase. We replace it with a concrete audit: 12 metrics, scored, before anyone commits. By the time we start building, we already know exactly what needs to change.</p>
  <h2>Concurrency beats sequence</h2>
  <p>Design and development don't have to be a relay race. On a proven stack we've shipped dozens of times, they run in parallel — you see a working draft within three days.</p>
  <h2>Two review rounds, not infinite</h2>
  <p>Open-ended feedback is where projects die. We structure exactly two rounds, anchored to a Figma design approved before code is written, so changes are cheap and decisive.</p>
  <h2>What we learned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scope creep is a process failure, not a client failure.</strong> Fix the process.</li>
    <li><strong>Speed and quality aren't opposites</strong> — slowness usually hides indecision.</li>
    <li><strong>Honesty closes more than pressure.</strong> Telling people the truth about their site builds trust faster than any sales call.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Seven days isn't a gimmick. It's the natural outcome of a system that refuses to waste anyone's time.</p>
</Post>
